Device Activity Tracking (Crucial for Understanding): The app must know exactly what app I am using on my phone, and the browser extension must know exactly what website I am on, on my laptop, and for how long. It is not enough to track total screen time; Gemini must know the specific apps (e.g., Instagram vs. a study app) and specific websites to cross-reference with the photos and understand my true state.

The Ledger: The Android app stores these contextual interpretations and device activity logs with timestamps. Over 24 hours, it accumulates a chronological timeline.

Live Snapshots: When the companion initiates a conversation or call, the app must capture a live photo immediately to see my facial expression, accompanied by the exact app/website I am currently on.

The Ears (Subconscious Listening & VAD)

The Rolling Buffer: The app must continuously listen to the microphone, maintaining a rolling, short-term audio buffer, constantly deleting older audio (like human working memory).

Keyword Triggers: The app must have a hardcoded list of ~100+ everyday keywords (food, emotions, relationships, activities). When a keyword hits, the app "wakes up," saves the surrounding 8–9 minutes of audio as an MP3, and sends it to Gemini to see what's going on.

Smart Intervention: If I set a goal to lose weight, and the app hears "burger," it calls me immediately. I tell it the truth—whether I was just talking about a burger, it misheard, or I was actually eating one.

Voice Activity Detection (VAD): We must integrate a local VAD (like Silero VAD) so the companion knows exactly when I am speaking versus background noise. This allows natural, turn-based calls. The companion speaks, waits for silence, and knows when it's my turn to reply.

The Brain (Contextual Reasoning & Memory)
Once the app has the contextual ledger, device activity logs, the saved MP3 clips, my goals, and my morning promises, it strings them together and sends them to Gemini. Gemini analyzes the audio tone, facial expression, and device context to understand the situation. It cross-references this with my broader memory (e.g., "He mentioned food, but he said he wanted to save money"). Gemini judges my reply based on the visual, auditory, and digital context.

Long-term Memory: Gemini has a 50-image/description limit per chat. When a chat hits 50 items, the extension starts a new chat, but saves the old chat link. To retrieve history, it sends the first 50 descriptions to the old chat, the next 50 to the new one, and so on. We are tracking and understanding my life for 6+ months with accurate data retrieval.

4. The Companion's Personality
Intervention, Not Notifications: If LifeAI sees I have been on my phone for 4.5 hours, it doesn't send a boring notification. It calls me. It says: "Okay, stay like this, it's your choice. But if you go for a walk right now, you will feel amazing." This is not random; it will only tell me to walk if that goal was added. If I have a study goal I haven't done, it will tell me to do that instead.

Negotiation: If I argue and say "Shut up, I'm busy," LifeAI uses the live photo and audio tone to judge my state. If I am genuinely stressed, it switches from arguing to negotiating: "Okay. I hear you. Let's make a deal. Just 5 minutes outside. No phone."

Unprompted Companionship: If I am walking silently and say, "Man, I'm so tired of this," the companion detects the speech via VAD and responds naturally, without me opening the app.

5. The Division of Labor: Gemini is the Brain, You Build the Body
The vast majority of the heavy lifting does not happen in the Android app. It happens in Gemini. Gemini understands context, remembers massive chat histories, and processes images and audio flawlessly. Because we use Gemini web chat as our brain, we do not need complex local AI models or NLP pipelines inside the Android app.

The Android app is just the sensory system and the hands.
All complex commands, situational judgment, arguing, negotiating, and memory synthesis come from Gemini.

The app acts as the subconscious. It quietly takes photos, listens to the rolling buffer, watches for keywords, and tracks device activity.

When a keyword hits, or it's time to evaluate, the app packages the contextual text, device activity logs, MP3 clips, and live photos, and sends them to Gemini.

Gemini decides what to do.

The app receives Gemini's command, makes the phone call, uses a free Text-to-Speech (TTS) model to speak Gemini's sentence, and uses VAD to listen to my reply.

My call conversation is recorded and sent back to Gemini web chat. Gemini judges my reply and decides what to do next. No data is useless.

